# Restore the USB gadget config that Motorola's init blanks -- this is what gives
# us adb. Without it the device NEVER enumerates on USB in Android, in any state.
#
# init.mmi.usb.rc deliberately blanks the config at load-bpf-programs:
#
#   on load-bpf-programs
#       setprop vendor.usb.config            ${sys.usb.config}
#       setprop persist.vendor.mot.usb.config ${persist.sys.usb.config:-" "}
#       setprop persist.sys.usb.config       " "        <-- blanked
#       setprop persist.vendor.usb.config    ${persist.sys.usb.config}
#
# and AOSP's init.usb.rc:109 then copies that blank forward:
#
#   on boot && property:persist.sys.usb.config=*
#       setprop sys.usb.config ${persist.sys.usb.config}
#
# Restoring the saved value is the job of /vendor/bin/init.mmi.usb.sh, which
# CANNOT RUN on this build:
#
#   E init: Could not start service 'vendor.mmi-usb-sh' as part of class
#     'late_start': File /vendor/bin/init.mmi.usb.sh (labeled
#     "u:object_r:vendor_file:s0") has incorrect label or no domain transition
#     from u:r:init:s0 to another SELinux domain defined.
#
# So sys.usb.config is never set, the UDC is never bound, and adbd waits forever
# on an endpoint that never comes up ("UsbFfs: connection terminated"), while
# android.hardware.usb.gadget-service.qti logs "Usb Gadget setcurrent functions
# failed". Setting sys.usb.configfs / sys.usb.controller / persist.vendor.usb.config
# from vendor.prop (27a4c6d) could not fix this: those are set BEFORE the blanking
# and are overwritten by it.
#
# TIMING IS THE WHOLE TRICK. From init.rc:
#     576  trigger post-fs-data
#     579  trigger load-bpf-programs      <-- the blanking happens here
#     588  trigger early-boot             <-- so we must restore HERE
#     589  trigger boot                   <-- ...before this reads it
# post-fs-data is too early (it runs before the blank) and boot is too late.
#
# The other two properties the configfs path needs are already handled by
# Motorola's own init, so they are deliberately NOT set here:
#   sys.usb.configfs=1        init.mmi.usb.rc:838, `on boot`
#   sys.usb.controller        init.mmi.usb.rc:245, from vendor.usb.controller
#                             (=a600000.dwc3 in build.prop)
# With sys.usb.config=adb, init.usb.configfs.rc:17-24 does the rest: start adbd,
# then on sys.usb.ffs.ready symlink ffs.adb into configs/b.1/f1 and write the UDC.
#
# (An earlier version forced "adb" here instead of restoring Motorola's saved
# value, on the grounds that a never-booted device has no saved value. That is
# now handled by the :-" " default below, which yields adb OFF on a wiped
# device -- official LineageOS semantics.)
#
# ⚠️ CORRECTION: this comment used to claim "Runtime mode changes (MTP etc.) still
# work: UsbDeviceManager sets sys.usb.config directly and does not go through this
# path." That was ASSERTED, never tested, and it was FALSE. Every USB mode change
# failed -- MTP, PTP, tethering and webcam -- because we were also building
# android.hardware.usb.gadget-service.qti, which made UsbDeviceManager choose
# UsbHandlerHal over UsbHandlerLegacy and stop setting sys.usb.config at all.
# Dropping that package (common.mk) restores Motorola's init-driven path, which is
# what stock uses and what the blocks in init.mmi.usb.rc are written for.
# RESTORE FROM MOTOROLA'S SAVED COPY, AT early-boot, INTO BOTH PROPERTIES.
#
# Official LineageOS semantics (vendor/lineage/config/common.mk: ro.adb.secure=1
# on userdebug, no persist.sys.usb.config default): USB debugging is OFF until
# the user turns it on, and the toggle persists. Stock does the same through
# init.mmi.usb.sh, which restores persist.vendor.usb.config from
# persist.vendor.mot.usb.config -- the value Motorola's blanking saved a moment
# earlier at load-bpf-programs (= last boot's persisted persist.sys.usb.config,
# or " " on a wiped device). That script cannot run here (unlabelled), so this
# does the same two writes.
#
# Why early-boot and why BOTH properties:
#   * load-bpf-programs (the blank) has already run; early-boot is the next
#     event, before `boot` copies persist.sys.usb.config into sys.usb.config
#     (init.usb.rc:109) and long before AdbService.systemReady reads it.
#   * Setting persist.vendor.usb.config here makes Motorola's own post-boot
#     property sweep (`on property:persist.vendor.usb.config=*`, which fires
#     once after `boot`) re-apply the RIGHT value instead of the blank. That
#     sweep is what used to turn the toggle off on some boots: it re-blanked
#     persist.sys.usb.config after a one-shot restore, racing
#     AdbService.systemReady. No second action is needed, so no race remains.
#
# History, both measured on this device (2026-09-13):
#   - forcing "adb" here alone: toggle came up OFF on ~4 of 6 boots (the sweep
#     re-blanked it before AdbService read it), port MTP-only, replug useless.
#   - forcing "adb" here AND on the sweep trigger: 9/9 boots ON -- but the user
#     could never turn debugging off persistently, which no official build does.
#
# Do NOT add any other writer of persist.sys.usb.config on boot-or-later paths;
# that reintroduces the AdbService race. The user's toggle is the only writer
# (UsbDeviceManager.setAdbEnabled: "adb" on, "" off), and it persists.
on early-boot
    setprop persist.vendor.usb.config ${persist.vendor.mot.usb.config:-" "}
    setprop persist.sys.usb.config ${persist.vendor.mot.usb.config:-" "}
